<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Composting Is Easy. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Composting Is Easy.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions on familiar topics. Interactive: Ask for repetition and clarification in a conversation about known topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions about recycling (e.g., "What can we recycle?" "We can recycle plastic."). Can ask for repetition, seek clarification, and confirm understanding in conversations about recycling instructions (e.g., "Could you please repeat that?" "Did you say paper and plastic bottles should go in different bins?"). Can summarize discussions about environmental actions (e.g., "We recycle to help the planet.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards or realia of different types of waste and recycling bins (e.g., a plastic bottle, a food scrap, a paper newspaper, a general trash can). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and elicits known words. The teacher then plays a short, distinct sound recording of items being sorted into different bins or a "compost crunching" sound. Students listen and guess what the sound is related to. The teacher introduces the words "recycle" and "compost" using visual cues and simple definitions, asking "Do you do this at home?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: "recycle" (/rɪˈsaɪkl/) ensuring the /r/ sound is clear, and "sustainability" (/səˌsteɪnəˈbɪlɪti/) focusing on the /s/ sound and segmenting the multisyllabic word. The teacher says, "We recycle plastic bottles. Listen and repeat: recycle." and "Composting is easy. Listen and repeat: easy." The teacher also models simple phrases like "We can recycle paper" and "There is a bin for food scraps." using clear, slow speech.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks comprehension of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a plastic bottle) "Can we put this in the trash?" (Expected answer: No!) "Where can we put it?" (Expected answer: In the recycling bin!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a banana peel) "Is a banana peel 'plastic' or 'food scraps'?" (Expected answer: Food scraps!) "Can we compost food scraps?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short dialogue (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) between two children (Ana and Tom) discussing recycling and composting at their school. The dialogue uses simple present tense, 'can', 'there is/are' and target vocabulary (plastic, bottles, compost, recycling, trash, food scraps, bin, help, environment, easy). Example dialogue snippet: "Ana: Hi Tom! What can we recycle here? Tom: We can recycle plastic bottles and paper. Ana: Oh, I see. What about food scraps? Tom: There is a special bin for compost. It's easy! We recycle to help the environment."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner demonstrates comprehension of the main points of short, simple oral texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the dialogue once. The teacher asks a general question: "What are Ana and Tom talking about?" (Expected answer: Recycling and composting). "Are they talking about school or home?" (Expected answer: School).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and answer True or False questions on a worksheet based on the dialogue. E.g., "Ana and Tom can recycle plastic bottles. (T/F)". "Composting is difficult. (T/F)". "There is a bin for food scraps. (T/F)".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information Matching):</b> Students listen one more time to match words from the dialogue (e.g., "plastic bottles", "paper", "food scraps") to pictures of their corresponding recycling/compost bins.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details, using present simple, modal verb "can", and 'there is/are' structures with target vocabulary (plastic, bottles, compost, recycling, trash, food scraps, bin, recycle, save, help, easy, environment).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listening Cloze:</b> The teacher plays a series of short sentences related to recycling and composting. Each sentence has one target vocabulary word missing. Students listen and write the missing word on a worksheet. E.g., "We can ___________ paper." (recycle). "There is a ___________ for food scraps." (bin/compost). "___________ bottles go in the blue bin." (Plastic).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing sentences and two picture options for each sentence. The teacher reads a sentence (e.g., "We recycle to help the environment."). Students listen and circle the correct picture (e.g., a picture of a clean planet vs. a polluted one). This focuses on listening for meaning and connecting it to visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Work – Listen and Sort:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with pictures of items (e.g., plastic bottle, banana peel, old newspaper, soda can, apple core). One student acts as the "speaker" and gives simple instructions based on a pre-written list (e.g., "Put the plastic bottle in the recycling bin.", "Compost the banana peel."). The other student acts as the "listener" and sorts the flashcards into designated "Recycle", "Compost", and "Trash" areas on their desk, based solely on what they hear. They then switch roles. This activity reinforces listening comprehension of instructions and vocabulary in context.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Role-Play: "Explaining Composting/Recycling Steps". Students will work in pairs to role-play a conversation where one student explains how to recycle or compost simple items, and the other student asks for repetition and clarification to ensure they understand the instructions. This directly addresses the interactive listening specific objective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into pairs. Each pair receives a scenario card (e.g., "You are explaining how to compost food scraps at home" or "You are explaining how to recycle paper and plastic bottles at school").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (explainer) describes 2-3 simple steps using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "First, we put food scraps in the compost bin." "Then, we add some leaves. It's easy!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (listener) actively listens and must ask at least two clarification or repetition questions using phrases like "Could you please repeat that?" or "Did you say paper and plastic bottles should go in different bins?" or "What can we recycle?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective use of target language and communicative strategies. Students are encouraged to use gestures and point to pictures if available.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on listening comprehension and the use of clarification questions. Exit ticket for individual comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Observation during Role-Play:</b> The teacher observes student pairs for their ability to ask for repetition/clarification and demonstrate understanding in their interactive conversations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket (Listening Comprehension):</b> The teacher plays a short audio clip (2-3 sentences) summarizing a simple recycling action (e.g., "We can recycle plastic bottles and newspapers to protect the environment."). Students listen and then write down 1-2 key things they heard about what we can recycle and why.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies main points from audio clips/partner explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Use:</b> Student uses appropriate phrases to ask for repetition and clarification (e.g., "Could you please repeat that?", "Did you say...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary & Grammar:</b> Understands target vocabulary (e.g., recycle, compost, plastic, food scraps, bin) and simple structures (present simple, can/could, there is/are).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you learned today about recycling or composting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy to understand your partner's instructions? Why or why not?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening carefully help you understand how to recycle or compost today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you can do at home this week to 'Recycle for Our World'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening activities were most effective for 5th graders at A1.3 level? Did the audio materials meet their comprehension level?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to successfully ask for repetition and clarification? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students struggling with this pragmatic function?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did the pronunciation practice (e.g., /r/ in recycle, /s/ in sustainability) support comprehension and retention?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and basic listening comprehension for recycling and composting. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or illustrated story that incorporates the same vocabulary and grammar structures, reinforcing their understanding in a new modality and providing more context for the "Recycling for Our World" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>